---
id: WC-003
type: rule
family: weak-cryptography
title: JWT configured with the `none` algorithm
version: 1

severity: warning
confidence: high
spectrum_position: 3

targets: [source]
file_extensions: [.ts, .tsx, .js, .jsx, .mjs, .cjs, .py, .java]
allowlist:
  [
    '**/*.test.ts',
    '**/*.test.tsx',
    '**/*.spec.ts',
    '**/*.spec.tsx',
    '**/__tests__/**',
    '**/test_*.py',
    '**/*_test.py',
    '**/tests/**',
    '**/explain/**',
  ]

detection:
  type: regex
  pattern: "\\balg(?:orithm)?s?[\"']?\\s*[:=]\\s*\\[?\\s*[\"']none[\"']"
  flags: i

related: [WC-001]
enabled: true
opt_in: false
---

You're wiring a JSON Web Token to the `none` algorithm. A token verified
with `none` has no signature to check — verification accepts any token
whose signature segment is empty, so anyone can mint one by editing the
claims. This is authentication turned off, not weakened.

On *verify*, pin an explicit algorithm allowlist and never include
`none`: `jwt.verify(raw, key, { algorithms: ['RS256'] })` /
`jwt.decode(raw, key, algorithms=['HS256'])`. Reject any token whose
header algorithm is not on your list — an attacker controls the header,
so trusting it is the vulnerability.

The only legitimate `none` use is an unsigned token you never treat as
authenticated (rare). If that is genuinely the case, keep it isolated
from any trust decision and suppress WC-003 with a reason.
